-- Q1: easy / L1 — PBGC insurance covers defined benefit plans only
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Which type of retirement plan is covered by the Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation (PBGC)?', '["Defined benefit plans", "Defined contribution plans", "Individual retirement accounts", "Simplified employee pension plans"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 401(a) and ERISA Title IV, the PBGC insures defined benefit plans, guaranteeing participants receive benefits up to a statutory limit if the plan terminates. Defined contribution plans such as 401(k)s are not covered because participants bear investment risk. Choice B is incorrect because there is no promised benefit amount for the PBGC to insure in a defined contribution arrangement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Graded vesting schedule calculation
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Pinnacle Solutions Inc. uses a 6-year graded vesting schedule for employer 401(k) matching contributions. Associate planner Kevin Torres has completed 3 full years of service and has $18,000 in employer matching contributions in his account. How much of the employer match is Kevin currently vested in?', '["$0, because graded vesting requires at least 4 years for any vesting", "$7,200, representing 40% vesting after 3 years", "$10,800, representing 60% vesting after 3 years", "$18,000, because all employer contributions vest immediately"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 411(a)(2)(B), the standard 6-year graded vesting schedule provides 20% after year 2, 40% after year 3, 60% after year 4, 80% after year 5, and 100% after year 6. Kevin''s 3 completed years yield 40% vesting: $18,000 x 40% = $7,200. Choice C ($10,800) is incorrect because it applies the year-4 rate of 60% rather than the correct year-3 rate of 40%.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — 529-to-Roth IRA rollover under SECURE 2.0
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Miranda Chen established a 529 plan for her son in 2008. The account has a $45,000 balance ($30,000 in contributions and $15,000 in earnings). Miranda wants to roll $7,000 from the 529 to her son''s Roth IRA in 2026. Which requirement must be met for this tax-free rollover?', '["The rollover is limited to $10,000 per year regardless of account age", "The 529 account must have been open for at least 10 years", "The 529 account must have been open for at least 15 years", "The beneficiary must be at least age 21 to receive the rollover"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under SECURE 2.0 Act Section 126, a 529-to-Roth IRA rollover requires the 529 account to have existed for at least 15 years, with annual transfers limited to the Roth IRA contribution limit and a $35,000 lifetime cap. Miranda''s 2008 account meets the 15-year threshold by 2026 (18 years). Choice B is incorrect because SECURE 2.0 specifies 15 years, not 10 years.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — SIMPLE IRA 25% early withdrawal penalty within first 2 years
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Brightfield Landscaping established a SIMPLE IRA plan in January 2025. Employee Rosa Martinez, age 38, began participating immediately. In March 2026, Rosa takes a $15,000 distribution to cover personal expenses. What early withdrawal penalty applies to this distribution?', '["No penalty because SIMPLE IRA distributions are always penalty-free", "A 10% early withdrawal penalty of $1,500", "A 15% early withdrawal penalty of $2,250", "A 25% early withdrawal penalty of $3,750"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 72(t)(6), distributions from a SIMPLE IRA within the first 2 years of participation are subject to a 25% early withdrawal penalty rather than the standard 10%. Rosa began participating in January 2025 and withdrew in March 2026 — only 14 months in. The penalty is $15,000 x 25% = $3,750. Choice B is incorrect because the standard 10% rate applies only after the 2-year participation period has elapsed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Spousal IRA contribution for nonworking spouse
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Victor and Diana Hernandez file jointly in 2026. Victor earns $95,000 in W-2 wages, while Diana has no earned income. Neither participates in an employer retirement plan. What is the maximum combined traditional IRA contribution for both spouses?', '["$14,000 ($7,000 each)", "$7,000 for Victor only, since Diana has no earned income", "$7,500 for Victor plus $3,500 for Diana", "$14,000 for Victor only in a single account"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 219(c), a spousal IRA allows a nonworking spouse to contribute up to the annual IRA limit ($7,000) as long as the couple files jointly and the working spouse has sufficient earned income to cover both contributions. Victor''s $95,000 easily supports $14,000 total. Choice B is incorrect because the spousal IRA provision specifically permits contributions for a spouse without earned income.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Roth conversion 5-year holding period per conversion
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Garrett Sullivan, age 52, converted $80,000 from his traditional IRA to a Roth IRA in 2024. In 2026, Garrett withdraws $80,000 from the Roth IRA. He has no other Roth IRA contributions. What are the tax consequences of the withdrawal?', '["The distribution is fully tax-free because conversions are treated as contributions", "The distribution is subject to ordinary income tax on the full $80,000", "The distribution is tax-free but subject to a 25% penalty", "The $80,000 is not subject to income tax but is subject to the 10% early withdrawal penalty"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 408A(d)(3)(F), converted amounts withdrawn within 5 years of conversion are subject to the 10% early withdrawal penalty if the taxpayer is under 59½. Garrett converted in 2024 and withdrew in 2026 — only 2 years later — while under 59½. The $80,000 is not taxed again (taxed at conversion) but the 10% penalty applies. Choice A is incorrect because the 5-year holding period per conversion has not been satisfied.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — SEPP method comparison for largest payment
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Cynthia Park, age 48, wants to take penalty-free distributions from her $600,000 traditional IRA under Section 72(t). She wants the largest possible annual distribution. Her CPA compares the three IRS-approved calculation methods. Which method will generally produce the largest annual payment?', '["The required minimum distribution method, which is recalculated each year", "The modified endowment contract method using mortality tables", "All three methods produce identical annual payment amounts", "The fixed amortization method, which uses a reasonable interest rate and life expectancy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 72(t)(2)(A)(iv) and IRS Notice 2022-6, the fixed amortization method generally produces the largest SEPP payments because it amortizes the account balance over life expectancy using a reasonable interest rate, yielding a higher fixed annual amount. Choice A is incorrect because the RMD method produces the smallest payments, as it divides only by the life expectancy factor rather than incorporating an interest rate component.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Cliff vesting forfeiture upon early departure
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Ridgeview Analytics uses a 3-year cliff vesting schedule for employer 401(k) matching contributions. Data analyst Megan Foster leaves the company after 2 years and 10 months of service. Her account shows $12,500 in employee deferrals and $8,400 in employer matching contributions. How much does Megan receive from the 401(k) upon separation?', '["$20,900, because all amounts vest after 2 years of service", "$12,500, because the employer match is forfeited under cliff vesting", "$16,700, because 50% of the employer match vests after 2 years", "$8,400, because only the employer match transfers upon separation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 411(a)(2)(A), 3-year cliff vesting provides 0% vesting until the employee completes 3 full years of service, then 100% vests immediately. Megan left at 2 years and 10 months — short of the 3-year cliff — so the $8,400 employer match is forfeited. Employee deferrals ($12,500) are always 100% vested. Choice C is incorrect because cliff vesting provides no partial vesting — it is all-or-nothing at the 3-year mark.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — SECURE 2.0 age-75 RMD trigger for born-1960+
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Financial planner Denise Albright advises client James Whitfield, born June 15, 1962, on when he must begin taking required minimum distributions from his traditional IRA. Under SECURE 2.0, what is James''s required beginning date?', '["April 1 of the year following the year he turns 70½", "April 1 of the year following the year he turns 73", "April 1 of the year following the year he turns 75", "April 1 of the year he turns 75"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under SECURE 2.0 Act Section 107, individuals born in 1960 or later must begin RMDs at age 75. James was born in 1962, so his RMD trigger age is 75 and his required beginning date is April 1 of the year following the year he turns 75. Choice B is incorrect because the age-73 threshold applies only to individuals born between 1951 and 1959, whereas those born in 1960 or later use the age-75 trigger.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Evaluate backdoor Roth with existing pre-tax IRA balances and 401(k) rollover strategy
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'CPA Nina Reeves advises Dr. Alan Prescott, who has a $400,000 traditional IRA (all pre-tax), earns $380,000 annually, and wants to implement a backdoor Roth conversion. Dr. Prescott also has a current employer 401(k) that accepts IRA rollovers. Nina is evaluating whether to (1) proceed with the backdoor Roth directly or (2) first roll the traditional IRA into the 401(k) before converting. Recommend the most tax-efficient strategy.', '["Proceed directly with the backdoor Roth, because the 401(k) rollover creates additional taxable events", "Skip the backdoor Roth entirely and invest in a taxable brokerage account instead", "Convert the entire $400,000 traditional IRA to a Roth at once and absorb the tax cost", "Roll the pre-tax IRA into the 401(k) first, then execute the backdoor Roth conversion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 408(d)(2), the pro-rata rule aggregates all traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRA balances when determining the taxable portion of a Roth conversion. If Dr. Prescott converts directly, nearly 100% of the $7,000 would be taxable due to the $400,000 pre-tax balance. Rolling the pre-tax IRA into the 401(k) — excluded from the pro-rata calculation — makes the conversion nearly tax-free. Choice A is incorrect because it ignores the pro-rata rule, which would make nearly the entire conversion taxable.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L2 — Employee deferrals always 100% immediately vested
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'New hire Aiden Brooks at Lakewood Consulting contributes $5,000 of his salary to the company''s 401(k) plan during his first year. After 8 months, Aiden resigns. The plan has a 6-year graded vesting schedule. How much of his $5,000 in employee deferrals can Aiden take with him?', '["$0, because he has not completed one full year of service", "$1,000, representing 20% vesting in year one", "$3,000, representing 60% vesting under graded vesting", "$5,000, because employee deferrals are always 100% immediately vested"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 411(a)(1) and ERISA Section 203(a), employee elective deferrals to a 401(k) plan are always 100% immediately vested regardless of years of service. Vesting schedules apply only to employer contributions such as matching or profit-sharing. Aiden keeps his full $5,000. Choice B is incorrect because the graded vesting schedule governs only the employer matching portion, not employee deferrals.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Coverdell ESA income phase-out calculation
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Single taxpayer Nathan Cross has a modified AGI of $105,000 in 2026 and wants to contribute to a Coverdell ESA for his niece. The full contribution phase-out range for single filers is $95,000 to $110,000. What is the maximum amount Nathan can contribute?', '["$667, based on the phase-out calculation for his income level", "$2,000, because the income limit applies only to married filers", "$0, because his income exceeds the $95,000 threshold entirely", "$1,000, representing a flat 50% reduction above $95,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 530(c), Coverdell ESA contributions phase out for single filers with MAGI between $95,000 and $110,000. Nathan''s $105,000 is $10,000 into the $15,000 range, or two-thirds through. His permitted contribution is $2,000 x (1 - 10,000/15,000) = $2,000 x 1/3 = $667. Choice C is incorrect because the phase-out begins at $95,000 but does not eliminate contributions entirely until $110,000.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — IRA contributions require earned income
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'What type of income is required to make a contribution to an individual retirement account (IRA)?', '["Investment income such as dividends and capital gains", "Earned income such as wages, salaries, or self-employment income", "Passive income from rental properties", "Any type of gross income reported on the tax return"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 219(b)(1), IRA contributions are limited to the lesser of the annual contribution limit or the taxpayer''s earned income (compensation). Earned income includes wages, salaries, tips, and net self-employment income. Choice A is incorrect because investment income such as dividends and capital gains does not qualify as compensation for IRA contribution purposes, unlike wages or self-employment income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluate 529 vs Coverdell for high-income family
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'CPA Sandra Ellison advises the Nakamura family, who have a 6-year-old daughter starting private K-12 school ($12,000/year tuition) and plan for college in 12 years. Their MAGI is $250,000 (MFJ). The Coverdell ESA income phase-out for MFJ ends at $220,000. Sandra is comparing a 529 plan only, a Coverdell ESA only, or both vehicles. Evaluate the most appropriate education savings strategy.', '["Coverdell ESA only, because it covers both K-12 and higher education expenses", "Both a 529 plan and a Coverdell ESA to maximize flexibility", "A 529 plan only, because the Nakamuras exceed the Coverdell ESA income phase-out", "Neither vehicle, because private K-12 tuition is not a qualified education expense"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 530(c), the Coverdell ESA contribution phases out completely for MFJ filers above $220,000 MAGI. The Nakamuras'' $250,000 MAGI makes Coverdell contributions impossible. The 529 plan has no income limits and permits up to $10,000/year for K-12 tuition under IRC Section 529(c)(7). Choice A is incorrect because the family is ineligible to contribute to a Coverdell regardless of its expense coverage breadth.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — 401(k) compensation cap effect on employer match
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Executive VP Heather Dalton earns $420,000 at Ridgeline Manufacturing in 2026. The company matches 401(k) contributions dollar-for-dollar up to 6% of compensation. The IRC Section 401(a)(17) compensation limit is $350,000. What is the maximum employer matching contribution Heather can receive?', '["$25,200, calculated as 6% of Heather''s full $420,000 salary", "$23,500, equal to the employee elective deferral limit", "$24,000, based on the maximum deferral for 2026", "$21,000, calculated as 6% of the $350,000 compensation cap"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 401(a)(17), employer contributions and matching calculations are capped at the annual compensation limit ($350,000). Even though Heather earns $420,000, the match is computed on the capped amount: $350,000 x 6% = $21,000. Choice A ($25,200) is incorrect because it applies the 6% match to the full salary rather than the statutory compensation cap that limits all plan calculations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: hard / L3 — Eligible designated beneficiary (disabled) stretch exception
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'In 2026, 70-year-old Margaret Townsend passes away with a $300,000 traditional IRA. Her sole beneficiary is her 45-year-old son Brian, who has been certified disabled under IRC Section 72(m)(7). Margaret had already begun taking RMDs. Under the SECURE Act, how must Brian distribute the inherited IRA?', '["Brian must distribute the entire account within 5 years of Margaret''s death", "Brian must distribute the entire account within 10 years of Margaret''s death", "Brian must take a lump-sum distribution because the decedent had begun RMDs", "Brian may stretch distributions over his own single life expectancy as an eligible designated beneficiary"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 401(a)(9)(E)(ii), a disabled beneficiary qualifies as an eligible designated beneficiary and is exempt from the SECURE Act''s 10-year rule. Brian may take annual RMDs stretched over his own single life expectancy, preserving tax deferral. Choice B is incorrect because the 10-year rule applies only to non-eligible designated beneficiaries, whereas disabled individuals qualify for the life-expectancy stretch exception.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L2 — 403(b) elective deferral limit matches 401(k)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Westbrook University professor Elena Ruiz, age 47, participates in the school''s 403(b) plan in 2026. What is the maximum amount Elena can contribute as an elective deferral?', '["$17,000, the SIMPLE IRA deferral limit", "$23,500, the same elective deferral limit as a 401(k) plan", "$7,000, the standard IRA contribution limit", "$16,500, the 403(b)-specific deferral limit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 403(b)(1) and Section 402(g), the 403(b) plan elective deferral limit is the same as the 401(k) limit — $23,500 for participants under age 50. Elena is 47 and not yet eligible for catch-up contributions. Choice A is incorrect because the $17,000 amount applies to SIMPLE IRA deferrals under IRC Section 408(p), not to 403(b) plans.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Form 8606 for nondeductible IRA basis tracking
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'In 2026, Rachel Kim contributes $7,000 to a traditional IRA. She is covered by her employer''s 401(k) and her single-filing MAGI of $92,000 exceeds the deductibility phase-out. The contribution is entirely nondeductible. What must Rachel file to track the after-tax basis of her contribution?', '["Form 5498, which the IRA custodian files automatically", "Schedule D to report the nondeductible contribution as a capital investment", "Form 8606 to report and track her nondeductible IRA basis", "No additional form is required because the IRA custodian records basis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 408(o)(4), a taxpayer who makes nondeductible contributions to a traditional IRA must file Form 8606 with their tax return to report and track the after-tax basis. This basis is critical for calculating the taxable portion of future distributions under the pro-rata rule. Choice A is incorrect because Form 5498 is filed by the custodian to report contributions to the IRS, however it does not track deductible versus nondeductible character.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — 529 plan contributions are not federally deductible
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'What is the federal income tax treatment of contributions made to a Section 529 education savings plan?', '["Contributions are not deductible for federal income tax purposes", "Contributions are fully deductible up to $7,000 per year", "Contributions are deductible only if the beneficiary is under age 18", "Contributions are deductible up to the annual gift tax exclusion amount"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 529(c)(1), contributions to a 529 plan are not deductible for federal income tax purposes. However, earnings grow tax-free and qualified distributions are excluded from gross income. Some states allow a state income tax deduction for 529 contributions, but there is no federal deduction. Choice B is incorrect because the $7,000 limit pertains to IRA contributions, not 529 plans.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Birth/adoption distribution exception ($5,000 per parent)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'On April 10, 2026, Mason and Andrea Powell welcome their first child. Mason, age 32, takes a $5,000 distribution from his traditional IRA, and Andrea, age 30, takes a $5,000 distribution from her separate traditional IRA. Neither qualifies for any other early withdrawal exception. What is the combined 10% early withdrawal penalty for both distributions?', '["$0, because the birth/adoption exception allows up to $5,000 per parent penalty-free", "$500, because only one spouse qualifies for the $5,000 exception", "$1,000, because the exception is limited to $5,000 per family", "$0, because all IRA distributions for child-related expenses are penalty-free"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 72(t)(2)(H), added by the SECURE Act, each parent may take up to $5,000 penalty-free from a retirement account within one year of a birth or adoption. The $5,000 limit applies per individual, not per family, so both Mason and Andrea each qualify. The combined penalty is $0. Choice C is incorrect because it misapplies a per-family cap — the statute provides a per-individual allowance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Roth 401(k) has no income limit for eligibility
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'What distinguishes a Roth 401(k) from a Roth IRA regarding eligibility?', '["A Roth 401(k) has lower annual contribution limits than a Roth IRA", "A Roth 401(k) has no income limit for employee eligibility, unlike the Roth IRA", "A Roth 401(k) provides tax-deductible contributions, unlike the Roth IRA", "A Roth 401(k) is exempt from the 5-year holding period for qualified distributions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 402A, the Roth 401(k) has no income limit for eligibility — any employee offered the plan can make Roth contributions regardless of MAGI. In contrast, under IRC Section 408A(c)(3), direct Roth IRA contributions phase out at higher income levels. Choice C is incorrect because neither Roth vehicle provides deductible contributions; both are funded with after-tax dollars.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L2 — 529 student loan repayment $10,000 lifetime limit
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'The Davenport family has $85,000 remaining in a 529 plan after their daughter completes her bachelor''s degree. She has $25,000 in student loan debt. What is the maximum amount that can be distributed tax-free from the 529 to repay student loans?', '["$25,000, because the full loan balance may be repaid from 529 funds", "$0, because student loan repayment is not a qualified 529 expense", "$10,000, the lifetime limit for student loan repayment from a 529 plan", "$35,000, matching the 529-to-Roth IRA rollover lifetime cap"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 529(c)(9), added by the SECURE Act, 529 plan distributions may be used for student loan repayment up to a $10,000 lifetime limit per borrower. Despite the $25,000 loan balance, only $10,000 qualifies as a tax-free distribution. Choice A is incorrect because the lifetime statutory cap limits the tax-free amount to $10,000 regardless of the outstanding loan balance.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Evaluate Roth 401(k) conversion when expecting lower future bracket
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'CPA Laura Finch advises Harold Greene, age 68, who has $1.2 million in a traditional 401(k) and approaches his RMD age of 73. Harold considers converting a substantial portion to the Roth 401(k) to reduce future RMDs, since SECURE 2.0 eliminated Roth 401(k) RMDs starting 2024. However, Harold expects to be in a lower tax bracket post-retirement. Evaluate whether the Roth conversion strategy is appropriate.', '["The conversion is not advisable because Harold expects a lower future tax bracket, making the upfront conversion tax cost exceed the RMD avoidance benefit", "The conversion is advisable because Roth 401(k) contributions are tax-deductible and avoid RMDs", "The conversion is not advisable because Roth 401(k)s remain subject to RMDs under current law", "The conversion is advisable because all converted amounts become tax-free immediately"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under SECURE 2.0 Section 325, Roth 401(k)s are exempt from RMDs starting 2024. However, Roth conversions are taxable under IRC Section 402A and are most beneficial when the taxpayer expects higher future rates. Since Harold expects a lower bracket post-retirement, paying a higher rate now to avoid lower-taxed RMDs is economically disadvantageous. Choice C is incorrect because SECURE 2.0 did eliminate Roth 401(k) RMDs starting 2024.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Recommend spousal inherited IRA strategy (treat as own vs beneficiary)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Financial planner Rebecca Torres advises 54-year-old Carla Jimenez, who inherited a $500,000 traditional IRA from her deceased husband in 2026. Carla has no immediate need for the funds and wants to maximize tax-deferred growth. She is weighing treating the inherited IRA as her own versus remaining as a beneficiary. Carla does not plan distributions before age 59½. Recommend the best approach.', '["Remain as beneficiary, because spousal beneficiaries are exempt from all RMDs", "Take a lump-sum distribution to avoid future RMD complications", "Remain as beneficiary, because it defers RMDs until the decedent would have turned 73", "Treat the IRA as her own, because it defers RMDs until Carla turns 73 and provides the longest growth period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 408(d)(3)(C), a surviving spouse may elect to treat an inherited IRA as their own, resetting the RMD timeline to the spouse''s own age-73 trigger. Since Carla is 54 and does not need distributions before 59½, treating it as her own defers RMDs for approximately 19 years. Choice C is incorrect because, although remaining as beneficiary defers RMDs based on the decedent''s age, Carla is younger and treating the IRA as her own provides greater deferral.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Defined contribution plan total annual addition calculation
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Harrison Wells, age 42, earns $180,000 at Crestwood Engineering in 2026. He contributes the maximum elective deferral of $23,500 to the 401(k), and Crestwood adds a 15% profit-sharing contribution. What is the total annual addition to Harrison''s account?', '["$23,500, because the annual addition is limited to the elective deferral amount", "$50,500, consisting of $23,500 in deferrals plus $27,000 in profit-sharing", "$70,000, the maximum total annual addition regardless of actual contributions", "$27,000, consisting only of the employer profit-sharing contribution"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 415(c), the total annual addition to a defined contribution plan is the sum of employee deferrals plus employer contributions, capped at the lesser of $70,000 or 100% of compensation. Harrison''s addition is $23,500 (deferral) + $27,000 (15% x $180,000 profit-sharing) = $50,500, which is under both limits. Choice C is incorrect because $70,000 is the statutory ceiling, whereas the actual addition is limited to the sum of contributions made.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Evaluate medical expense exception with 7.5% AGI floor
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'CPA Thomas Grant advises 42-year-old Maria Espinoza, who incurred $38,000 in unreimbursed medical expenses in 2026. Maria''s AGI is $200,000. She wants to take $38,000 from her traditional IRA and asks whether the medical expense exception to the 10% early withdrawal penalty applies. Evaluate the tax consequences of the distribution.', '["The full $38,000 is exempt from the 10% penalty because the medical expenses exceed 7.5% of AGI", "No portion qualifies for the exception because IRA distributions cannot be used for medical expenses", "Only $23,000 is exempt from the 10% penalty, and the remaining $15,000 is subject to the penalty", "The full $38,000 is subject to the 10% penalty because the exception applies only to employer plans"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 72(t)(2)(B), the early withdrawal penalty exception applies only to medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI. Maria''s threshold is $200,000 x 7.5% = $15,000. Her qualifying excess is $38,000 - $15,000 = $23,000. The remaining $15,000 is subject to the 10% penalty ($1,500). Choice A is incorrect because the exception does not exempt the full distribution — rather than covering all medical expenses, it applies only to the amount exceeding the 7.5% AGI floor.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Coverdell ESA age-30 distribution deadline
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Coverdell ESA beneficiary Tyler Marsh turns 30 on September 15, 2026. The account has a $12,000 balance and Tyler has no remaining qualified education expenses. What happens to the account if Tyler takes no action before his 30th birthday?', '["The remaining balance must be distributed within 30 days, with earnings subject to income tax and a 10% penalty", "The account continues tax-free until Tyler reaches age 59½", "The balance is automatically rolled over into a traditional IRA", "The balance is forfeited to the state because Coverdell ESAs expire at age 30"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 530(b)(1)(E), a Coverdell ESA must be fully distributed within 30 days after the beneficiary reaches age 30 (unless special needs apply). If no qualified expenses remain, the earnings portion is subject to ordinary income tax plus a 10% additional tax under Section 530(d)(4). Choice B is incorrect because Coverdell ESAs have a statutory age-30 deadline and do not continue like a retirement account.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — SECURE 2.0 reduced RMD excise tax to 25%
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'Under SECURE 2.0, what is the excise tax rate for failing to take a required minimum distribution from a traditional IRA?', '["50% of the shortfall amount", "25% of the shortfall amount, reduced to 10% if corrected within 2 years", "10% of the shortfall amount with no reduction available", "No penalty, because SECURE 2.0 eliminated RMD penalties"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under SECURE 2.0 Act Section 302, the excise tax for failure to take an RMD was reduced from 50% to 25% of the shortfall. If the taxpayer corrects the shortfall within the applicable correction window (generally 2 years), the penalty is further reduced to 10%. Choice A is incorrect because the 50% rate was the pre-SECURE 2.0 penalty; however, Section 302 reduced it to 25% with a 10% corrective option.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Disability exception applies to both employer plans and IRAs
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'At age 53, investment banker Paul Whitaker suffers a permanent disability preventing him from working. He has $350,000 in his employer''s 401(k) and $120,000 in a traditional IRA. Paul needs $200,000 for living expenses. His CPA analyzes which accounts qualify for the 10% early withdrawal penalty exception. What is the correct analysis?', '["Both the 401(k) and IRA distributions qualify for the disability exception under Section 72(t)(2)(A)(iii)", "The 401(k) qualifies for the age-55 separation exception, but the IRA distribution is subject to the penalty", "Neither distribution qualifies for an exception because Paul is under age 55", "Only the IRA qualifies for the disability exception; the 401(k) requires age 59½"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 72(t)(2)(A)(iii), the disability exception applies to distributions from both employer plans and IRAs when the taxpayer is permanently and totally disabled as defined in Section 72(m)(7). Paul''s permanent disability qualifies him for penalty-free distributions from both accounts regardless of age. Choice B is incorrect because the age-55 separation exception requires actual separation at 55+, which Paul has not reached.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Traditional IRA fully deductible with no employer plan
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', 'What is the IRA deductibility rule for a taxpayer who is not an active participant in any employer-sponsored retirement plan?', '["The contribution is never deductible regardless of income level", "The contribution is deductible only if MAGI is below $79,000", "The contribution is fully deductible regardless of income level", "The contribution is deductible only up to 50% of the annual limit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 219(g), the MAGI-based phase-out of traditional IRA deductibility applies only to taxpayers who are active participants in an employer plan (or whose spouse is). A taxpayer with no employer plan coverage may deduct the full IRA contribution regardless of income. Choice B is incorrect because the $79,000 phase-out range applies only to active participants, whereas a non-participant has no income restriction on deductibility.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
